{"name":"Megadrive Screen","hex":"#4a40ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"screen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A physical barrier that shelters or hides.\nA physical divider intended to block an area from view, or provide shelter from something dangerous.","A physical divider intended to block an area from view, or provide shelter from something dangerous."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To filter by passing through a screen.","To filter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/screen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sega Genesis","blocks":[{"text":"The Sega Genesis, known as the Mega Drive outside North America, is a 16-bit fourth generation home video game console developed and sold by Sega. It was Sega's third console and the successor to the Master System. Sega released it in October 1988 in Japan as the Mega Drive, and in August 1989 in North America as the Genesis. In 1990, it was distributed as the Mega Drive by Virgin Mastertronic in Europe, Sega Ozisoft in Australasia, and Tectoy in Brazil. In South Korea, it was distributed by Samsung Electronics as the Super Gam*Boy and later the Super Aladdin Boy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Sega-Mega-Drive-JP-Mk1-Console-Set.jpg/330px-Sega-Mega-Drive-JP-Mk1-Console-Set.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sega_Genesis","wikidata":"Q10676"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Sega-Genesis-Mk2-6button.jpg/500px-Sega-Genesis-Mk2-6button.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Sega-Genesis-Mk2-6button.jpg/1280px-Sega-Genesis-Mk2-6button.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Sega-Genesis-Mk2-6button.jpg/500px-Sega-Genesis-Mk2-6bu